<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sbgn xmlns="http://sbgn.org/libsbgn/0.3" xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0">
  <map id="QBM-05" language="process description">
    <extension>
      <qbo:mapNotes xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0">TCA cycle and the Fe/S-dependent steps. The review's Figure 4 renders this as a linear chain from glucose to oxaloacetate with no OAA-to-citrate closure, which the draft's own margin note flags. The closure matters: it is what shows that the two Fe/S-dependent steps throttle flux around the whole loop instead of interrupting a line. Aconitase and succinate dehydrogenase are the review's sentinel nodes and are annotated once on QBM-01, referenced here rather than re-declared, so one entity cannot carry different evidence on two maps. The citrate-to-isocitrate ratio is the most direct available in-vivo proxy for Fe/S cluster integrity and is worth measuring in any near-null-field metabolomics experiment. Note that plant mitochondrial fumarase is a class-II enzyme with no Fe/S cluster, unlike the class-I bacterial form it is sometimes confused with. Derived from: Figure 4 of Porterfield &amp; Barker, "Plant Responses to Near-Null Magnetic Fields" — redrawn as a closed cycle, expanded to gene level and bound to Arabidopsis loci</qbo:mapNotes>
    </extension>
    <glyph id="mitochondrial_matrix" class="compartment">
      <label text="Mitochondrial matrix" />
      <bbox x="-26.00" y="-26.00" w="718.46" h="447.36" />
    </glyph>
