    </glyph>
    <glyph id="LIPOYL_SYNTHASE" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="mitochondrial_matrix">
      <label text="Lipoyl synthase" />
      <bbox x="221.66" y="234.08" w="123.31" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="LIPOYL_SYNTHASE__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="radical SAM" />
        <bbox x="227.66" y="225.08" w="77.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:LIPOYL_SYNTHASE" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_4Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_donor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G20860</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">2.8.1.8</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>A radical-SAM enzyme generating a 5'-deoxyadenosyl radical from a [4Fe-4S] cluster — genuine organic radical chemistry on an iron-sulfur centre, which is the tightest coupling of the review's two proposed mechanisms found anywhere in primary metabolism. Its product lipoylates pyruvate dehydrogenase and the 2-oxoglutarate dehydrogenase complex, so a defect propagates straight into TCA flux.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>The radical is generated inside the active site and quenched within one turnover; whether a spin-correlated pair with a long enough lifetime to be field-sensitive ever forms is untested.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="COMPLEX_II" class="complex">
      <label text="SDH (complex II)" />
      <bbox x="378.96" y="227.68" w="154.93" h="60.24" />
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_II__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="FAD" />
        <bbox x="384.96" y="218.68" w="30.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <glyph id="COMPLEX_II__u1" class="unit of information">
        <label text="3 × Fe/S" />
        <bbox x="384.96" y="218.68" w="56.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:COMPLEX_II" evidenceTier="T3" confidence="inferred">
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_2Fe2S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_4Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>iron_sulfur_3Fe4S</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>ros_leak_site</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>57Fe</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>33S</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:57692</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G66760</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT3G27380</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G40650</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT5G09600</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT2G46505</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005749</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="ec">1.3.5.1</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:rationale>Carries all three cluster types plus a covalent flavin, and sits at the junction the review nominates as the bottleneck — a change here propagates into both TCA flux and respiratory electron flow.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
